The demonstration presents the completed work clearly and confidently. A presentation of ten to twelve slides covers the title and team, problem statement, objectives, comparison of the existing and proposed systems, architecture, technology stack, modules, live demo or screenshots, testing results, challenges and solutions, conclusion, and future scope. The live demo, lasting three to five minutes, shows logging in as an administrator, registering a student, running the camera to mark attendance, viewing the record on the dashboard, and exporting a report. A backup video or screenshots should be kept ready in case of technical problems. For the question-and-answer session, the team should be prepared to explain why the technology was chosen, how the system handles poor lighting and look-alike faces, how user data is protected, what its limitations are, how it could scale, and what the biggest challenge was during development.
